Engine: how the last battle ended, "CRES" (open fix 57).

STATUS: APPLIED 30 September 2026 by work order 194 (Part B; reserved by
work order 193) — orion2re f1e6c581 on orionlayer-local (before: f506bc6c), this
file's diff byte for byte (index lines included). Required by
tools/version_check.py since work order 194.

It was applied, from ~/orion2re on the orionlayer-local branch, with:

    patch -p1 < ~/orionlayerv3/doc/ext_combat_result.patch

and a rebuild. It comes back off with `git revert f1e6c581` or `patch -R -p1`,
and a rebuild.

WHAT IT IS FOR

    A battle's end has no box and no input (COMBINIT::End_Of_Combat_), so a
    client saw the result only afterwards, as a changed _ship[] (work order
    193, survey §3). ext::Record_Combat_Result, called from
    COMBINIT::Tactical_Combat_ just before End_Of_Combat_ writes the losses
    back, keeps: a result number, the battle's serial (0 when the battle
    was aborted before it started — abort_tactical), the winner exactly as
    handed to End_Of_Combat_ (1 attacker, -1 defender, also after the
    50-turn timeout, 666 both gone), the combat turn, star, colony, sides,
    the colony's population lost, the stardate, and per unit its ship
    index, owner, previous owner, status, retreat and capture flags and
    damage. Block "CRES" is written on ANY screen while a result is kept,
    and it is kept until the next battle starts: the stardate moves on in
    the same Next_Turn_ as the battles (nextturn.cpp:123, :161), possibly
    before any tick, so "until the turn moves on" could have sent it never.

    What is recorded is a copy of values End_Of_Combat_ receives or reads;
    nothing the game reads is written. An engine without OrionLayer behaves
    exactly as before. One marker, `OrionLayer, open fix 57.`, on one line
    at every changed place (ext_api.h, ext_api.cpp ×2, combinit.cpp).

